## 2. How the page selects which service it shows

**A query string: `?date=YYYY-MM-DD`.** The date *is* the Firestore document ID.

`public\service-builder.js:703-711`
```js
const urlParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
this.date = urlParams.get('date');
this.shell = urlParams.get('shell');
if (this.shell === 'mobile') document.body.classList.add('shell-mobile');
if (!this.date) {
    window.location.href = this.shell === 'mobile' ? 'mobile.html#/calendar' : 'service-calendar.html';
    return;
}
await this.load();
```

Other recognised params: `&shell=mobile` (line 705) and `&validate=true` (line 723).

The load and the live listener both key straight off `this.date`:
- `public\service-builder.js:811` — `const doc = await db.collection('services').doc(this.date).get();`
- `public\service-builder.js:1434` — `const serviceRef = db.collection('services').doc(this.date);`
- `public\service-builder.js:1520` — `db.collection('services').doc(this.date).onSnapshot(...)`

Title rendering: `public\service-builder.html:218` → `x-text="formatDate(date)"`, backed by `public\service-builder.js:1957-1959` → `DateUtils.formatDateLong(dateStr)`.

**There is no client-side router and no store holding "current service".** Changing the date means a full page navigation to `service-builder.html?date=<other>` — which matters for point 6.

Every inbound link builds that URL by hand:
- `public\service-calendar.js:1022`, `:1237`
- `public\calendar-event.js:1723` (`servicesHref`)
- `public\recurring-events.js:1272` (`orderOfServiceHref`)
- `public\index.html:650, 673, 690`
- `public\analytics.html:373, 468`; `public\peoples-page.html:819`
- `public\service-guide-editor.html:107`

