## 5. Data model

- **Collection:** `services`, top-level Firestore. Firestore rules: `firestore.rules:315-318`
  ```
  match /services/{dateId} {
    allow read: if true;
    allow create, update, delete: if isEditor();
  }
  ```
- **Date field:** there isn't one — **the document ID *is* the date**, `YYYY-MM-DD`, local calendar date. `CONTEXT.md:405-406` — *"### Service — A liturgical event (usually a Sunday service), identified by its date (YYYY-MM-DD)."* Confirmed by `public\service-builder.js:811` (`.doc(this.date)`) and the explicit warning at `public\mobile\data.js:982-984`. Some docs carry a redundant `date` field (`public\mobile\data.js:257` reads `d.date || doc.id`) but it is not queryable/reliable.
- **Date helpers:** `public\date-utils.js` — `toDateStr`, `todayStr`, `parseDateStr`, `addDays(dateStr, n)`, `addWeek(dateStr)` (line 41-43: `return addDays(dateStr, 7);`), `formatDateLong`, `upcomingSundays`. Explicitly local-time, never `toISOString()` (see the header comment, lines 1-12). Loaded in the page at `public\service-builder.html:1279`.
- **No draft/published status.** There is no `status`, `published`, or `draft` field anywhere on a Service. The nearest things:
  - `guideSystem: 'v2' | 'legacy'` — `public\service-builder.js:422`, `public\guide-store.js:194-209`. Which booklet generator the week uses.
  - `service.guide` — the frozen guide snapshot (large; the reason not to fetch the whole collection).
  - `isIrregular` / `irregularElements` — a non-standard liturgy shape (`public\service-builder.js:382-383`).
  - `hasBaptism`, `removedHymns`, `theme`, `keyVerse`, `liturgy.*`, person refs (`preacher`/`preacherId`, `serviceLeader`, `musicLeader`, `musicHelpers`) — `public\service-builder.js:825-856`, `CONTEXT.md:405-414`.
- **Existence is the only "state":** `public\service-builder.js:817` sets `this._docExists = doc.exists;`, and a never-saved Sunday is a valid, openable page (opening one writes nothing). So "navigable services" is effectively "every Sunday", not "every doc in `services`" — matching how both list surfaces enumerate Sundays arithmetically. **Decide which of those two universes the arrows step through** — `date ± 7 days` (matches the calendar's model, always lands somewhere valid) vs. a `documentId()` range query for the next *existing* doc (skips blank weeks).

---

## 6. Unsaved-edit state — yes, and it matters a lot here

This page is a **live autosaving editor** with a `beforeunload` guard, and every prev/next arrow would be a full document navigation.

- Dirty tracking: `public\service-builder.js:626-628`
  ```js
  get isDirty() {
      return this.originalService !== JSON.stringify(this.service);
  }
  ```
  (`originalService` declared at `:357`, set on load at `:893`, reset after save at `:1460`.)
- Autosave debounce, **3 seconds**: `public\service-builder.js:1488-1503, 1573-1582`
  ```js
  watchForChanges() { this.$watch('service', () => this.scheduleSave()); },
  ...
  scheduleSave() {
      if (!this.canEdit || this.saving) return;
      clearTimeout(this._saveTimer);
      this._saveTimer = setTimeout(() => {
          if (this.isDirty && !this.saving) this.save();
      }, 3000);
  },
  ```
  The comment at `:1489-1493` says 3 s (rather than the elder documents' 1.5 s) because the save also settles involvement + fairness.
- **The navigation blocker:** `public\service-builder.js:727-732`
  ```js
  window.addEventListener('beforeunload', (e) => {
      if (this.canEdit && this.isDirty) {
          e.preventDefault();
          e.returnValue = '';
      }
  });
  ```
  → An arrow that just sets `location.href` will pop the browser's "Leave site?" dialog whenever there is a pending edit within the 3 s window. The arrow handler should `await this.save()` (or cancel `_saveTimer` and flush) before navigating.
- Live remote sync also runs on this page and is keyed to `this.date`: `watchRemoteChanges()` / `adoptRemoteChanges()` at `:1505-1571`, subscription at `:1520`, unsubscribe handle `_remoteUnsubscribe` at `:1515`. **Nothing ever calls `_remoteUnsubscribe`** — fine today (page navigation tears it down), but an in-place SPA-style date switch would leak listeners.
- Presence/box-locking is also date-scoped: `watchPresence()` (`public\service-builder.js:697`), `public\service-presence.js`.
- Header UI already surfaces dirty state — `public\service-builder.html:222-231` (`#header-dirty-pill` "Unsaved Changes" / "All Changes Saved") and the Save button at `:234-238`. That's the row where `< >` would naturally sit, alongside `m-header__actions` (or in `m-header__lead` next to the title at `:217-219`).
- The page also has an in-page tab state that back-navigation already special-cases: `tab: 'order' | 'roles'` (`public\service-builder.js:344`, `openTab` at `:642-649`, `listenForShellBack` at `:651-666`). Arrows should decide whether moving to the next Sunday preserves the active tab.
- Governing decision: `docs\adr\0032-a-page-saves-itself-a-dialog-does-not.md` (page editors autosave). Also relevant: `docs\adr\0034-a-sunday-saves-the-fields-you-changed.md`.

**Existing tests that pin this page's behaviour** (these will need updating/extending):
`test\service-builder-live.test.js`, `test\service-roles-tab.test.js`, `test\service-field-level-save.test.js`, `test\autosave.test.js`, `test\service-authorship.test.js`, `test\calendar-pages.test.js` (asserts exact hrefs, e.g. `:562` `'service-builder.html?date=2026-07-12'`), `test\page-script-deps.test.js`, `test\page-header-width.test.js`, `test\date-utils.test.js`.

Findings.

## 1. Header: mobile vs desktop

**Desktop header** — `public/service-builder.html:210-258`, a direct child of `<body>` (body opens at :156 with `x-data="serviceForm()"`, so the header is inside the Alpine scope):
- :213 back link `<a :href="shell === 'mobile' ? 'mobile.html#/calendar' : 'service-calendar.html'" class="m-back">` with label "Services"
- :218 `<h1 class="m-header__title m-header__title--serif" x-text="formatDate(date)"></h1>` — **this is the only place the service date is rendered as text on the whole page.** Grep for `x-text="…date…"` in the file returns exactly one hit (:218). The service-header card comment at :263-264 says so explicitly: *"The date is the page's title and lives in the bar."*
- :222-233 dirty pill (`id="header-dirty-pill"`) and an unnamed "All Changes Saved" success badge, both inside `.m-header__actions`
- :236 `id="header-save-btn"`

**On mobile the whole desktop header is display:none'd**, by `public/mobile-shell-header.js:270-276`:
```js
document.querySelectorAll(cfg.hideSelector || "body > header").forEach(function (el) {
  if (!adopted) adopted = el.querySelector(".m-header__actions");
  el.style.display = "none";
});
```
`hideSelector` is not set by this page (`public/service-builder.html:26` is just `{ title: 'Service Editor', onBack: true }`), so the default `body > header` matches the tool header and hides it, taking the `<h1>` date with it. There is also `public/mobile-shell.css:9-11` (`html.shell-mobile header { padding-top: … }`) and page CSS `public/service-builder.html:89-90`, but those only pad — the actual hide is the inline `style.display = "none"` above.

**Answer to the specific question: the service date is NOT visible anywhere in the mobile header, or anywhere else on the page.** The mobile header title is the literal string `'Service Editor'` (`mobile-shell-header.js:311-314`, `title.textContent = cfg.title`), and nothing on this page calls `window.setMobileHeaderTitle` (grep for `setMobileHeaderTitle` across `public/` hits only its definition at `mobile-shell-header.js:321`). So a phone user editing a Sunday sees no date at the top at all.

One subtlety worth knowing: `.m-header__actions` is **moved** (adopted), not copied, into the mobile header row (`mobile-shell-header.js:271, 316`). Of the three things inside it, `public/service-builder.html:98-99` hides two:
```css
body.shell-mobile #header-save-btn,
body.shell-mobile #header-dirty-pill { display: none !important; }
```
The "All Changes Saved" badge (:227-231) has no id, so it survives and renders in the mobile header row. Save itself moves to a docked bottom bar `#mobile-save-bar` (`public/service-builder.html:96-112` CSS, markup at :1296-1313), shown only under `body.shell-mobile`.

`body.shell-mobile` is set twice: `public/mobile-shell.js:75` (`markBody`) and again in `public/service-builder.js:706` (`if (this.shell === 'mobile') document.body.classList.add('shell-mobile')`). `html.shell-mobile` is set at `public/mobile-shell.js:44` and is what gates `mobile-shell-header.js` at all (:27 early-return).

## 2. What back actually does

`public/service-builder.js:651-666`:
```js
listenForShellBack() {
    if (typeof document === 'undefined' || !document.addEventListener) return;
    document.addEventListener('mobile-header:back', () => {
        if (this.tab !== 'order') { this.tab = 'order'; return; }
        window.location.href = 'mobile.html#/calendar';
    });
}
```
Called first thing in `init()` (`service-builder.js:668-669`).

The event comes from `public/mobile-shell-header.js:290-292`: because `onBack: true` is set, the chevron handler does `document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("mobile-header:back"))` and returns — it never touches `history`.

So the **shell header's back arrow**: first press closes the Roles tab (`tab = 'order'`); if already on the order tab, it does a **forward navigation** via `location.href` to `mobile.html#/calendar`. It is not `history.back()`.

The **phone's hardware/gesture back** is a different path and does **not** reach that listener. `public/mobile/app.js:386-399` registers the Capacitor `backButton` listener and defines `M._back` (drawer → `history.back()` → `exitApp`), but that lives in `mobile.html`'s Preact app only. `service-builder.html` is a separate document; grep for `backButton`/`Capacitor.Plugins` across `public/*.js` and `public/mobile/*.js` shows no listener on any shell page. So on a shell page Android back falls through to Capacitor's default WebView handling (`history.back()`, exit at the bottom of the stack) — which, since `mobile.html` → builder was a `location.href` navigation (`public/mobile/app.js:258`), pops back to the mobile app, bypassing the "close the Roles tab first" rule entirely. The two back affordances therefore behave differently.

Note also the `beforeunload` guard at `public/service-builder.js:727-731` — leaving dirty (including via the shell back arrow's `location.href`) triggers the browser's unsaved-changes prompt.

## 3. History manipulation

**None on this page or in the shell.** Repo-wide grep over `public/*.js` and `public/mobile/*.js` for `pushState|replaceState|popstate|hashchange` returns exactly one hit: `public/mobile/app.js:377-378`, a `hashchange` listener for the Preact router. No `pushState`/`replaceState` anywhere in the codebase.

Consequences:
- Routing inside `mobile.html` is done by assigning `location.hash` (`public/mobile/app.js:257, 272, 273, 274, 417`). Each hash assignment is a normal history entry, so back retraces mobile routes one at a time.
- `service-builder.js` navigates only via `window.location.href` (:664, :708). Grep for `location.replace` on this page: none.
- **Yes — if the page navigated to another `?date=` with `location.href`, every date visited would become its own history entry, and the phone's back gesture would retrace them one Sunday at a time** before finally reaching `mobile.html`. (`location.replace` or `history.replaceState` would be needed to avoid that; neither is used anywhere today.)

## 4. Can a Sunday be cancelled / skipped / moved?

The machinery exists generally, and CONTEXT.md's claim is about the **UI**, which holds — with one caveat.

- Model: `public/events-occurrence-core.js:372-388` — `notHappening(o)` is `o.cancelled === true || !!o.movedTo`; `movedNote` at :435-442 renders "Not happening" / "Moved to …".
- Skip write: `public/events-store.js:580-613` `cancelOccurrence(db, seriesId, date, cancelled, options)` — **no `sunday_service` guard at all.** It would happily write `{cancelled:true}` to `event_occurrences/sunday_service_<date>` (collections at `events-store.js:33-35`: `SERIES='events'`, `OCCURRENCES='event_occurrences'`).
- Move write: `public/events-store.js:673-679` **does** guard explicitly:
  ```js
  if (seriesId === Core.SUNDAY_SERVICE_ID) {
      throw new Error('The Sunday Service cannot be moved this way: its order of service lives '
          + 'under its own date, and moving the Event would split one Sunday across two.');
  }
  ```
- Delete: `events-store.js:632-643` refuses any date of a series outright.
- UI: the only caller of `cancelOccurrence` in the whole repo is `public/calendar-event.js:317-338` (`skipThisOne()`), and its button (`public/calendar-event.html:735-737`) sits inside `<section x-show="isEditor && patternEditable">` (:676). `patternEditable` is `public/calendar-event.js:767-769`: `this.isEditor && !!this.series && !this.isSunday`. `canMove` (:782-791) also excludes Sunday. The section's own comment (`calendar-event.html:671-675`) states the reason. So **no "Skip this one", no "Move this one", no pattern controls on a Sunday** — CONTEXT.md:545 is accurate as a statement about the UI.

**But the Sunday's non-weekliness is not enforced by the data model, only by the absence of UI:**
- `SUNDAY_RULE` is `Object.freeze({ freq:'weekly', weekday:0, startDate:'2023-01-01' })` at `public/events-store.js:221-227`, used only as a *fallback*: `recurrenceFor(series)` at :229-233 returns `series.recurrence` **first** and falls back to `SUNDAY_RULE` only when the stored `events/sunday_service` doc has no `recurrence` field. A `recurrence` written onto that document would win.
- `reconcileSundayService` (`public/events-core.js:152+`, called from `events-store.js:998-1008` and `scripts/seed-events.js`) restores id/name/locked/roleSlugs — it does not assert or restore the recurrence.
- `cancelOccurrence` has no Sunday guard, so a `cancelled` flag could be written for a Sunday date programmatically; `EventsOccurrenceCore.notHappening` would then report that Sunday as not happening on the Calendar/Event pages.
- Crucially for this page: **the Order of Service surfaces do not read cancellation at all.** Grep for `cancelled|notHappening|movedTo` in `public/service-calendar.js`, `public/mobile/screens-content.js` and `public/mobile/data.js` → zero hits. `service-calendar.js` generates dates by `current.setDate(current.getDate() + 7)` from a hardcoded start; `screens-content.js` walks `addDays(dd, 7)`. So even a cancelled Sunday would still appear as an editable slot here.

Net: for the purposes of a prev/next-Sunday control on this page, **stepping ±7 days is exactly what both existing Services lists do** — no exception machinery is consulted by either. The theoretical non-weekly cases (a `recurrence` written on `events/sunday_service`, or a `cancelled` occurrence) are unreachable from the UI and invisible to the Services lists.

## 5. First-Sunday and horizon constants

**No shared constant. Hardcoded in-place, and duplicated.**

`public/service-calendar.js:511-523` (inside the `DOMContentLoaded` handler):
```js
const startDate = new Date(2023, 6, 9); // July 9, 2023 (Month is 0-indexed)
const endDate = new Date();
endDate.setFullYear(endDate.getFullYear() + 2);
allSundays = [];
let current = new Date(startDate);
while (current <= endDate) { allSundays.push(new Date(current)); current.setDate(current.getDate() + 7); }
```
(The `// Ensure we start on a Sunday` comment at :519 is stale — there is no snapping code; it relies on 2023-07-09 being a Sunday.)

Repo-wide grep for `2023, 6, 9` / `2023-07-09` / `FIRST_SUNDAY`:
- `public/service-calendar.js:512` — the calendar bound
- `public/docx-importer.js:139` — `const projectStartDate = new Date(2023, 6, 9);`, a second independent copy used to reject imports before the project start
- No `FIRST_SUNDAY` / `PROJECT_START` constant exists anywhere.

The "+2 years" is likewise local; the only other `setFullYear(+2)` is `public/calendar-view.js:311-313`, an unrelated window for the "next few dates" chips under a recurrence sentence.

`SUNDAY_RULE.startDate` (`public/events-store.js:226`) is `'2023-01-01'`, deliberately earlier and snapped forward by the weekly rule — so the events system and the Services calendar do not share an anchor either.

**Mobile Services screen uses completely different bounds** — `public/mobile/screens-content.js:380-403`, `CalendarScreen`:
